A||||7||fiero56||Factory cruise installation||||3||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-14-2000||11:09 PM||||I have decided that I would rather install a factory cruise system to my ('88)Formula(v-6 5-speed), rather than go aftermarket. Suppose its because I am not an "aftermarket" kind of person. I know that adding factory cruise will be a much larger undertaking than going aftermarket, but I would prefer to keep this car close to factory specs. I don't have a helms factory manual, yet, but need to know everything that is "cruise specific". Meaning, what will need to be added, and what will need to be swapped.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||theogre||11-14-2000||11:27 PM||||For V6 and the non DIS 4 aftermarket cruise is allot closer to OE than you may think. The older OE setup also uses an external cruise module. (The cruise function is built into the DIS ECM) If you dress it into the harness neat then only show judges would know the dif. Plus you'll have all new parts that won't fail in some very annoying, posibly dangerous, ways.<P>The couple I've seen, unfortunatly in other cars, and according to those here that have used aftermarket units they come with the OE style turn stalk. Other than figuring out just where to tie into the VSS signal they are pretty much a drop in.<P>Only thing I can't tell you is how the aftermarket servo ties to the throttle. Haven't seen the aftermarket fiero one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-14-2000||11:33 PM||||The factory servo is kinda' what I'm looking keep, appearance wise anyways, and I'm no ricer. If it's on the car, it better be functional.<P>I have wondered how aftermarket plugs into throttle too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||11-15-2000||06:52 AM||||I bought the JC Whitney unit and it looks exactly like the factory unit on the inside of the car.  The throttle connection is via cable similar to the factory unit.  The servo looks very similar to the factory unit also.  <P>It gets it's VSS signal by tapping into the wire at the ecm connector.  It also ties into the brake light circuit and monitors the rate of rise of RPMs to sense disengaging the clutch or slipping the trans into neutral.<P>I haven't installed it yet.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierov8||11-15-2000||09:58 AM||||Fiero56!<P>If you are interested - I have the cable, servo, and coffee can from my 86 that's available.  Wiring and the cruise control module is all that's needed after that.<BR>It's in extremely good condition!  Removed it -- don't need it cluttering up my V swap :-)<P>Email for more details,<P>-jeff d<BR>Fort Worth, TX<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||11-15-2000||12:21 PM||||Fierov8:<P>I might take you up on that offer. I need to see what my finances look like right now(I should start keeping track of how much I have), and what I can afford. I will definitely be adding cruise before the end of the year, hopefully sooner. What are you looking to get for them? Post here or email me, you know my adress(fiero56@siscom.net). BTW, check is in the mail. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||11-15-2000||05:55 PM||||Carrolles:<P>Can you tell me which JCW cruise unit you purchased?  They list several in their catalog.  I am interested in adding this to my daughter's 87 Coupe.<P>Ed Parks adviced me not to fool with a factory setup, he says much harder to install compared to a good aftermarket unit.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||11-17-2000||06:32 AM||||Bob,<P>I'm currently on an out of town assignment and don't have the info with me.  I'll be going home at Thanksgiving and will look it up for you.<P>I don't remember what model it is but it was an OEM look-alike with the VSS pickup instead of the magnetic pickup.  It does look noce and shouldn't be hard to install.  It comes with a bag full of gadgets to choose from and instructions for connecting the cable to the throttle body.  You will route a small wiring harness through the firewall.  They recommend drilling a small hole near where the ECM harness bulkhead connector is and installing a grommet.<P>It recommends using a vacuum canister for small displacement engines.  I got a plastic spherical one out of a late model Grand Am at the junk yard.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
